=========================================================
noexcept (C++11) – Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is noexcept?
---------------------
- Introduced in C++11.
- Specifies that a function will NOT throw exceptions.
- If an exception escapes a noexcept function, std::terminate() is called.

Syntax:
-------
void func() noexcept
{
    // No exceptions should escape
}

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why use noexcept?
--------------------
• Documents that a function never throws.
• Enables compiler optimizations.
• Improves performance of STL containers.
• Important for move constructors and move assignment operators.

---------------------------------------------------------

3. noexcept and Move Semantics (Most Important)
-----------------------------------------------
Mark move constructor and move assignment as noexcept.

class A
{
public:
    A(A&&) noexcept {}
    A& operator=(A&&) noexcept = default;
};

Why?

- During reallocation, std::vector prefers MOVE only if it is noexcept.
- Otherwise it may COPY elements to provide strong exception safety.

Interview Answer:
"STL containers prefer noexcept move constructors because if moving throws
during reallocation, the container could be left in an inconsistent state."

---------------------------------------------------------

4. Checking noexcept
--------------------

void foo() noexcept {}

void bar() {}

cout << noexcept(foo()) << endl;   // true
cout << noexcept(bar()) << endl;   // false

noexcept(expression)

Returns:
true  -> expression cannot throw
false -> expression may throw

---------------------------------------------------------

5. Conditional noexcept
-----------------------

template<typename T>
void swap(T& a, T& b)
noexcept(noexcept(std::swap(a,b)))
{
    std::swap(a,b);
}

Used in generic/template code.

---------------------------------------------------------

6. What if noexcept function throws?
------------------------------------

void func() noexcept
{
    throw runtime_error("Error");
}

Result:
std::terminate() is called immediately.*********************************

---------------------------------------------------------

7. noexcept vs throw()
----------------------

Old C++98:
void func() throw();

Modern C++11:
void func() noexcept;

Use noexcept.
throw() is deprecated (C++11) and removed in C++17.

---------------------------------------------------------

8. Interview Questions
----------------------

Q1. What is noexcept?
A:
Specifies that a function will not throw exceptions.

Q2. What happens if a noexcept function throws?
A:
std::terminate() is called immediately.

Q3. Why should move constructors be noexcept?
A:
STL containers like std::vector prefer moving elements only when move
operations are noexcept. Otherwise they may copy elements instead.

Q: How does noexcept improve performance?**********************************************

A:

"The biggest performance benefit is that STL containers such as std::vector
can safely use move constructors during reallocation. Moving objects is
typically much cheaper than copying them. Additionally, because the compiler
knows a noexcept function cannot throw, it can perform certain optimizations
such as simplifying exception-handling code."

=========================================================
Interview Keyword
=========================================================

noexcept
        ↓
Move is guaranteed not to throw
        ↓
std::vector chooses MOVE
        ↓
Less copying
        ↓
Better performance

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Key Interview Points
-----------------------

✓ Introduced in C++11.
✓ Indicates a function will not throw exceptions.
✓ If violated → std::terminate().
✓ Important for move constructor and move assignment.
✓ std::vector prefers noexcept move over copy.
✓ Use noexcept(expression) in template code.
✓ Replaces old throw() exception specification.

---------------------------------------------------------

10. One-Line Interview Answer
-----------------------------

"noexcept is a C++11 feature that guarantees a function will not throw
exceptions. If it does, std::terminate() is called. It is especially
important for move constructors because STL containers such as std::vector
prefer noexcept move operations during reallocation, improving performance
and exception safety."
